content.text:
The network counters for search and geo are flat: no errors, and I/O matches normal. That points away from a network fault and toward a delay held inside search before it sends the geo request. I have enough to conclude.

```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "search",
      "fault_kind": "http_slow",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,2) avg_ms, round(max(duration)/1e6,2) max_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name IN ('frontend','search') AND span_name IN ('HTTP /hotels','search.Search/Nearby','geo.Geo/Nearby') GROUP BY 1,2,3,4 UNION ALL SELECT 'norm', service_name, span_name, attr_span_kind, count(*), round(avg(duration)/1e6,2), round(max(duration)/1e6,2) FROM normal_traces WHERE service_name IN ('frontend','search') AND span_name IN ('HTTP /hotels','search.Search/Nearby','geo.Geo/Nearby') GROUP BY 1,2,3,4", "claim": "Search's outgoing geo.Geo/Nearby client span rose from about 0.5 ms to about 1527 ms; /hotels rose from 6 ms to 1527 ms."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, count(*) n, round(avg(epoch_ms(s.time) - epoch_ms(c.time)),1) start_gap_ms, round(avg((epoch_ms(c.time)+c.duration/1e6) - (epoch_ms(s.time)+s.duration/1e6)),1) end_gap_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON s.parent_span_id=c.span_id AND s.trace_id=c.trace_id WHERE c.service_name='search' AND c.span_name='geo.Geo/Nearby'", "claim": "Geo's server span starts about 1529 ms after search's client span; geo's own handling takes 0.15 ms."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, span_name, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,2) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='search' AND span_name IN ('rate.Rate/GetRates','geo.Geo/Nearby') GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "Search's other outgoing call, rate.Rate/GetRates, stays at about 3.7 ms, so only the geo path is slowed."},
        {"kind": "metric", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, service_name, metric, attr_k8s_pod_name pod, count(*) n, round(avg(value),1) avg_v, round(max(value),1) max_v FROM abnormal_metrics_sum WHERE service_name IN ('search','geo') AND metric IN ('k8s.pod.network.errors','k8s.pod.network.io') GROUP BY 1,2,3,4", "claim": "Search and geo pod network errors stay at zero and network I/O is unchanged, so no transport-level fault is visible."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {
      "from": "search",
      "to": "frontend",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT 'abn' src, service_name, span_name, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,2) avg_ms, round(max(duration)/1e6,2) max_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='frontend' AND span_name IN ('HTTP /hotels','search.Search/Nearby') GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "Frontend's /hotels server span (about 1527 ms) and its search.Search/Nearby client span (about 1524 ms) both inherit search's delay."}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Summary:** The user-visible degradation is confined to the `/hotels` endpoint on `frontend`. Its latency went from about 6 ms to about 1.5 s. Other frontend paths (`/cinema`, `/museums`, `/recommendations`, `/restaurants`) are unchanged.

The chain is `search` → `frontend` → user. The `search` service's outgoing `geo.Geo/Nearby` calls are the only slow path. The geo server span is still about 0.15 ms, while the search client span is about 1.5 s. The geo span starts about 1.5 s after the client span, so the time is spent before geo handles the request. Search's other outgoing call (`rate.Rate/GetRates`) stays normal, which points to a fault on that one call path. Pod restarts, CPU, memory and pod network counters are all normal, so I classified this as `http_slow` at search rather than a network fault. The absence of network-level changes is the main reason; there are no per-connection hubble metrics in this case to rule out a network fault completely.
